Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:01:08 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] cmake: build libigc and libigdfcl on macOS (Mach-O, ld64)

IGC's CMake knows Windows, Linux and Android. macOS is built as Linux
(the same POSIX user-mode code: dlopen, pthreads, the LINUX/ICBE_LINUX
definitions, LLVM_ON_UNIX), with the ELF/GNU-linker specifics replaced:

- architecture detection: Darwin is a "Linux" OS; the processor comes
  from CMAKE_OSX_ARCHITECTURES when set (an x86_64 build on an arm64
  Mac), and the host is the target (a Mac runs what it builds, natively
  or under Rosetta), so no cross-compilation is set up
- ld64 has no --whole-archive/--start-group: the archives whose members
  are only reached at run time (the BiF resources of libigc, the OpenCL
  C header resource of libigdfcl, found by dlsym) are linked with
  -force_load (igc_link_whole_archive); ld64 resolves archives over the
  whole link line without groups
- the GNU/ELF-only options are removed from the compile and link flags:
  -Bsymbolic (a two-level-namespace dylib binds its own references
  already), -z relro/now/noexecstack (dyld's defaults), --no-undefined
  (ld64's default; -undefined dynamic_lookup, which the clang branch
  adds, would turn it off) and --gc-sections (-dead_strip instead);
  no --exclude-libs
- the version script that keeps LLVM's symbols out of libigc's exports
  becomes an exported-symbols list made by
  Scripts/igc_create_exports_list_macos.sh: the same selection (IGC's
  default-visibility definitions and the BiF resources) read with nm -m

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
